---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { MAINTAINER } from '../consts.ts';

/**
 * CLAUDE.md §9: the About page is a trust instrument and an SEO asset at once.
 * It must state who runs this, disclose the maintainer's affiliation plainly,
 * describe the method, and give a working corrections route.
 *
 * TODO(chris): fill the MAINTAINER constants and re-read this page end to end
 * before the site is public. Do not launch with placeholder text.
 */

const lastUpdated = '2026-08-16';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About and methodology"
  description="Who builds this guide, how candidates are covered, how sources are handled, and how to request a correction."
  lastUpdated={lastUpdated}
>
  <h1>About this guide</h1>

  <p class="lede">
    This guide exists because an ordinary voter looking up a school board or judicial race
    in Livingston County finds almost nothing. It covers every candidate for every office
    on the ballot, by the same standard, with a source for every claim.
  </p>

  <h2>Who builds this</h2>
  <p>
    This guide is built and maintained by {MAINTAINER.name}.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Disclosure:</strong> {MAINTAINER.name} is a {MAINTAINER.role}. That is stated
    here plainly rather than buried, because you should be able to judge this guide knowing
    who made it. The response to that disclosure is not a promise to have no opinions — it
    is a method designed so that opinions cannot get into the content, and so that you can
    check whether they did.
  </p>

  <h2>How every candidate is treated the same</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Identical structure.</strong> Every candidate in a race gets the same fields
      in the same order, and the same word budget. Equal structure removes the main lever
      of bias, which is not lying — it is emphasis.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Their words first.</strong> What a candidate says about themselves comes from
      their own campaign materials and is attributed as their description, not restated as
      established fact.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>No verdicts.</strong> This guide never calls a candidate best, strongest, most
      qualified, or concerning. It never scores, rates, ranks, or assigns anyone a political
      lean. It presents; you decide.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Missing information is stated, not implied.</strong> "No campaign website
      found" and "Did not respond" appear in the same words for every candidate, including
      ones the maintainer personally supports. A short entry reflects what is publicly
      available, not a judgment about the candidate.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Everyone is invited.</strong> Every candidate in every covered race is
      contacted with the same offer to submit or verify their information and photo.
      Who was contacted, and who replied, is recorded on each race page.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>How sources work</h2>
  <p>
    Every substantive claim carries a link to a primary source, in this order of preference:
    the candidate's own campaign materials, official campaign-finance filings, government
    and court records, then on-the-record reporting. Facts are date-stamped, because a job
    title, a donation total, or an endorsement can go stale.
  </p>

  <h2>How unflattering facts are handled</h2>
  <p>
    Neutrality here means applying one standard to everyone, not avoiding anything
    uncomfortable. A fact about a candidate is included only if it passes all three tests:
    it comes from a verifiable public record; it bears on the office being sought rather
    than the candidate's private life; and the equivalent fact would be included for a
    candidate of any party. Facts that pass are stated flatly, with the source and without
    adjectives. Rumor, inference, and anything drawn from a candidate's family or personal
    life are not included at all.
  </p>

  <h2>What this guide will not do</h2>
  <p>
    No endorsements. No recommendations. No "vote for" language. No candidate scores or
    ideology ratings. No voter targeting or campaign operations of any kind. This guide is
    not affiliated with, and does not coordinate with, any campaign, party, or committee.
  </p>

  <h2>Corrections</h2>
  <p>
    If something here is wrong, incomplete, or out of date — and especially if you are a
    candidate whose information is misstated — write to
    <a href={`mailto:${MAINTAINER.email}`}>{MAINTAINER.email}</a>. Corrections are made
    promptly. Any candidate may submit or verify their own statement and photo at any time.
  </p>
  <p>
    The full content of this guide, and every change ever made to it, is public and
    auditable in its source repository. You do not have to take the neutrality claim on
    faith; you can read the diffs.
  </p>
</BaseLayout>
